/**
\page usage Usage examples

Short examples for the five samplers. They use `Real = double`; \ref usage_float shows the
`float` instantiation. Every sampler is used the same way: construct, call `define(...)`, then
call `operator()` once per sample. \ref usage_oblique and \ref usage_pic are complete programs.

\tableofcontents

\section usage_build Building the tests and the demo

The headers need only a C++11 compiler. To build and run the code in `cpp/`:

\code{.sh}
make -C cpp test                  # regression suite; exit status 0 if every check passes
cd cpp && make && ./main.exe      # short worked examples of all five samplers
make -C cpp CXX=clang++ test      # use a different compiler
\endcode

\section usage_bikappa Bi-Kappa velocities

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_kappa_distribution.H"
#include <fstream>
using Real = double;
using BK = bi_kappa_distribution<Real>;

BK biKappa;
//             kappa  theta_perp  theta_par  ub          cap            seed
biKappa.define(2.0,   1.0,        2.0,       {0, 0, 1},  BK::no_cap(),  20030410);

std::ofstream out("samples_bikappa.txt");
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) {
    BK::point_type v = biKappa();          // {vx, vy, vz} in the global frame
    out << v[0] << ' ' << v[1] << ' ' << v[2] << '\n';
}
\endcode

The seed is the sixth argument, so the cap has to be given when a seed is passed to
`define(...)`. Alternatively, call `biKappa.seed(20030410)` after `define(...)`.

\subsection usage_bikappa_cap The velocity cap

The fifth argument, `max_normalized_velocity`, is a cap in thermal speeds. It defaults to 20.

\code{.cpp}
BK dist;
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1});                // cap = 20 (default)
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, BK::no_cap());  // no cap
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, 50.0);          // cap = 50
bool c = dist.param().capped();                       // true for a finite cap
dist.max_normalized_velocity(BK::no_cap());           // remove the cap
dist.max_normalized_velocity(0.0);                    // throws std::invalid_argument
\endcode

With a finite cap, a draw is redrawn if `|v_i| / theta_i > cap` for any component in
field-aligned coordinates. The samples then follow the bi-Kappa distribution conditioned on
that box, not the bi-Kappa distribution itself. For `kappa <= 3/2` a cap of 20 removes an
appreciable part of the high-energy tail; the paper's section on finite velocity bounds
quantifies this. Use `no_cap()` when the tail matters.

The box is aligned with the field-aligned axes and rotates with `ub`. Global-frame components
are bounded individually only when `ub = {0, 0, 1}`; the speed is always bounded by
`cap * sqrt(2 theta_perp^2 + theta_par^2)`. See \ref params_cap for details.

\section usage_bimaxwell Bi-Maxwellian velocities

The same interface without `kappa`. The cap works as for `bi_kappa_distribution`, with the same
default of 20; for a Maxwellian it has no practical effect.

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_maxwellian_distribution.H"
using BM = bi_maxwellian_distribution<double>;

BM biMaxwell;
biMaxwell.define(1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1});                          // theta_perp, theta_par, ub
biMaxwell.define(1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, BM::no_cap(), 20030410);  // no cap, with a seed
BM::point_type v = biMaxwell();
\endcode

\section usage_reproducible Seeding

\code{.cpp}
BK dist;
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0);
dist.seed(55555);      // s >= 0: the same seed gives the same sequence
dist.seed(-1);         // s < 0: seed from std::random_device
\endcode

Without a seed, runs are not reproducible. On every sampler `seed(s)` also calls `reset()`, which
discards any variate cached from earlier draws, so reseeding restarts the stream exactly. For
`bi_kappa_distribution` a given seed gives the same values under libc++ and libstdc++ on the same
architecture; the values are not bitwise identical across architectures. The other four samplers
use `std::normal_distribution` or `std::uniform_real_distribution`, whose values can depend on
the standard library. CHANGELOG.md lists the versions in which the values for a given seed changed.

\section usage_external_rng Sharing one engine

Pass your own engine to `operator()` to draw several samplers from one stream. For
`bi_kappa_distribution` the engine must produce a power-of-two number of distinct values, as
`std::mt19937` and `std::mt19937_64` do. This is not checked at compile time: `std::minstd_rand`
compiles without a warning but does not meet the requirement.

\code{.cpp}
#include <random>
std::mt19937 gen(42u);

BK core, halo;
core.define(5.0, 1.0, 1.0);
halo.define(2.0, 3.0, 3.0);

BK::point_type a = core(gen);   // both draw from gen
BK::point_type b = halo(gen);
\endcode

\section usage_oblique An oblique magnetic field

`ub` can point in any direction and need not have unit length. `theta_par` applies along `ub`
and `theta_perp` across it. The sampler returns velocities in the global frame, so to recover
field-aligned components, project onto the unit vector `b = ub / |ub|`: `v_par = v . b` and
`v_perp^2 = |v|^2 - v_par^2`. This program checks the result against the variances of
\ref params_theta, which exist for `kappa > 3/2`.

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_kappa_distribution.H"
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdio>

int main()
{
    using BK = bi_kappa_distribution<double>;
    const BK::point_type ub = {1.0, 2.0, 3.0};   // field direction, any length

    BK dist;
    //          kappa  theta_perp  theta_par  ub  cap           seed
    dist.define(4.0,   1.0,        2.0,       ub, BK::no_cap(), 12345);

    const double norm = std::sqrt(ub[0] * ub[0] + ub[1] * ub[1] + ub[2] * ub[2]);
    const double b[3] = {ub[0] / norm, ub[1] / norm, ub[2] / norm};

    const int n = 1000000;
    double sum_par2 = 0.0, sum_perp2 = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const BK::point_type v = dist();                           // global frame
        const double v_par = v[0] * b[0] + v[1] * b[1] + v[2] * b[2];
        const double v2 = v[0] * v[0] + v[1] * v[1] + v[2] * v[2];
        sum_par2 += v_par * v_par;
        sum_perp2 += v2 - v_par * v_par;
    }
    // Expected: <v_par^2> = kappa theta_par^2 / (2 kappa - 3) = 3.2 and
    //           <v_perp^2> = 2 kappa theta_perp^2 / (2 kappa - 3) = 1.6.
    std::printf("<v_par^2> = %.3f  <v_perp^2> = %.3f\n", sum_par2 / n, sum_perp2 / n);
    return 0;
}
\endcode

The two perpendicular axes the sampler uses internally matter only for a finite cap, whose box
is aligned with them; see \ref params_cap.

\section usage_float Single precision

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_kappa_distribution.H"
using BKf = bi_kappa_distribution<float>;

BKf distf;
distf.define(3.0f, 1.0f, 2.0f, {0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f}, BKf::no_cap(), 12345);
BKf::point_type vf = distf();   // std::array<float, 3>
\endcode

`bi_kappa_distribution<float>` does all of its arithmetic in `float`, so for the same seed it
returns different numbers from the `double` instantiation. It is suitable when kappa is not
close to 1/2 and about seven significant digits per component are enough. Near kappa = 1/2 a
noticeable fraction of `float` draws exceeds the largest `float` (about `1.4e-4` of draws at
kappa = 0.55); see \ref params_precision and use `double` there.

\section usage_pic Loading particles in a simulation

This program fills arrays for `N` particles with a drifting bi-Kappa population, as a
particle-in-cell code would at initialization. It converts kinetic temperatures to thermal
speeds with the relation of \ref params_theta, which holds for `kappa > 3/2`; for smaller kappa,
specify `theta` directly. Each thread or MPI rank has its own sampler, and so its own engine,
with its own seed. The ranks are simulated here by a loop.

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_kappa_distribution.H"
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdio>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    using BK = bi_kappa_distribution<double>;

    const double k_B = 1.380649e-23;             // J/K
    const double m = 1.67262192e-27;             // proton mass, kg
    const double kappa = 3.0;
    const double T_perp = 1.0e5, T_par = 2.0e5;  // kinetic temperatures, K
    const BK::point_type b = {0.0, 0.6, 0.8};    // field direction, unit length
    const double drift[3] = {4.0e5, 0.0, 0.0};   // bulk velocity, m/s

    // theta = sqrt((2 - 3/kappa) k_B T / m), valid for kappa > 3/2.
    const double theta_perp = std::sqrt((2.0 - 3.0 / kappa) * k_B * T_perp / m);
    const double theta_par = std::sqrt((2.0 - 3.0 / kappa) * k_B * T_par / m);

    const int n_ranks = 4;
    const int n_per_rank = 250000;
    const int base_seed = 1000;                  // change it to get a different run
    const int n = n_ranks * n_per_rank;
    std::vector<double> vx(n), vy(n), vz(n);

    for (int rank = 0; rank < n_ranks; ++rank) {   // in a real code, one rank or thread each
        BK dist;
        // no_cap(): sample the bi-Kappa distribution itself; the default cap of 20 thermal
        // speeds would sample it conditioned on a box instead.
        dist.define(kappa, theta_perp, theta_par, b, BK::no_cap(), base_seed + rank);
        for (int i = rank * n_per_rank; i < (rank + 1) * n_per_rank; ++i) {
            const BK::point_type v = dist();       // global frame, zero mean
            vx[i] = v[0] + drift[0];
            vy[i] = v[1] + drift[1];
            vz[i] = v[2] + drift[2];
        }
    }

    // Check: the kinetic temperature along b, m <(v - drift) . b)^2> / k_B, is close to T_par.
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const double v_par = (vx[i] - drift[0]) * b[0] + (vy[i] - drift[1]) * b[1] +
                             (vz[i] - drift[2]) * b[2];
        sum += v_par * v_par;
    }
    std::printf("T_par from the sample: %.4g K (target %.4g K)\n", m * sum / n / k_B, T_par);
    return 0;
}
\endcode

\section usage_redefine Changing parameters

`define(...)` replaces the whole configuration. Each single-parameter setter re-validates the
full set.

\code{.cpp}
BK dist;
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 1.0);
dist.kappa(5.0);
dist.theta_par(2.5);
dist.ub({1, 2, 3});
dist.max_normalized_velocity(BK::no_cap());
\endcode

\section usage_overflow Overflow near kappa = 1/2

Close to `kappa = 1/2`, some bi-Kappa draws exceed the largest representable floating-point
number. Without a cap they are returned with infinite components; with a cap they are redrawn.
`n_nonfinite()` counts such draws and `n_attempts()` counts all attempts since the last
`define()`, `seed()` or `reset()`.

\code{.cpp}
BK dist;
dist.define(0.505, 1.0, 1.0, {0, 0, 1}, BK::no_cap());
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) {
    BK::point_type v = dist();
    if (!std::isfinite(v[0]) || !std::isfinite(v[1]) || !std::isfinite(v[2])) {
        // handle or discard the draw
    }
}
unsigned long long lost = dist.n_nonfinite();
\endcode

Under `-ffast-math` the compiler may assume that no infinity occurs, so `no_cap()` and the
counters are unreliable. `bi_kappa_distribution.H` then prints a compile-time note; define
`BI_KAPPA_ALLOW_FAST_MATH` to silence it. See \ref params_precision for typical rates.

\section usage_velocity Isotropic velocities from your own density

`general_velocity_generator` draws `w = |v|^2` from a density `g(w)` you supply, by rejection
sampling, and gives the velocity an isotropic direction with speed `sqrt(w)`. There is no mass
argument: for a distribution written in energy, use `w = 2E/m`.

\code{.cpp}
#include "general_velocity_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;

// Maxwellian with thermal speed 1, written as a density in w = |v|^2.
auto gW = [](Real w) -> Real { return std::sqrt(w) * std::exp(-w); };

general_velocity_generator<Real> velGen;
//            g    w_min  w_max
velGen.define(gW,  0.0,   40.0);
velGen.seed(20030410);
general_velocity_generator<Real>::point_type v = velGen();
\endcode

If `w_min == w_max`, every sample has speed `sqrt(w_min)`. `probe_points` (fourth argument,
default 1024) must be at least 64.

\section usage_field_aligned Field-aligned velocities

`field_aligned_velocity_generator` draws `w = v_par^2` from your density `g(w)` and puts the
parallel component along `ub` (`sign = +1`) or opposite to it (`sign = -1`). The two
perpendicular components are Maxwellian with thermal speed `theta_perp`, each
`N(0, theta_perp^2 / 2)`, and each is capped at `20 * theta_perp` by default. The result is
returned in the global frame.

\code{.cpp}
#include "field_aligned_velocity_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;
using FA = field_aligned_velocity_generator<Real>;

auto gW = [](Real w) -> Real { return std::exp(-0.5 * w); };   // density in w = v_par^2

FA faGen;
//           g    w_min  w_max  theta_perp  ub          sign
faGen.define(gW,  0.0,   40.0,  1.0,        {0, 0, 1},  +1);
faGen.seed(20030410);
FA::point_type v = faGen();

// Without the perpendicular cap:
faGen.define(gW, 0.0, 40.0, 1.0, {0, 0, 1}, +1, 1024, 100000, FA::no_cap());
\endcode

\section usage_position Positions from your own density

`general_position_generator` draws positions in one to three dimensions from a density
`rho(x)` in an axis-aligned box, by rejection sampling. The point is a `std::vector<Real>` of
length `dimension`.

\code{.cpp}
#include "general_position_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;
using pos_t = general_position_generator<Real>::point_type;   // std::vector<Real>

auto rho = [](const pos_t& x) -> Real { return 1.0 + std::sin(x[0]) * std::sin(x[1]); };

general_position_generator<Real> posGen;
const Real pi = 3.14159265358979;
//            dim  lower        upper       density
posGen.define(2,   {-pi, -pi},  {pi, pi},   rho);
posGen.seed(20030410);
pos_t x = posGen();
\endcode

\section usage_errors Errors

- `define(...)` and the setters throw `std::invalid_argument` for a value outside its valid
  range (see \ref parameters).
- The general, field-aligned and position generators throw `std::runtime_error` if called
  before `define(...)`, if `max_reject_tries` is exhausted, or if your function returns a
  negative or non-finite value.
- `bi_kappa_distribution` and `bi_maxwellian_distribution` throw `std::runtime_error` after
  10^6 consecutive rejections by the cap.

\code{.cpp}
try {
    general_velocity_generator<Real> g;
    general_velocity_generator<Real>::point_type v = g();   // not defined yet: throws
} catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
    // handle the error
}
\endcode

\section usage_full Demo program

`cpp/main.cpp` runs a short example of each sampler:

\include main.cpp
*/
